 system and the  fact that  many children are removed um when they  shouldn't be removed and more so from  black and brown families than white  families so that's another conversation  but we the systematic race of an  institutional racism that exists  in in our world is alive and well in  foster care and adoption today  uh just and there's no way around that  which means we have to talk about  these differences of race and we have to  talk about you know helping families  stay together  and when there is no other way but to  have an adoption take place then that is  okay  but to do so at the highest level of  engagement with as much information  and as much real scrutiny quite frankly  as possible so that these children have  what we are promising which is physical  emotional and psychological safety of a  family  to to support and love them  april what have we not talked about um i  know we're running out of time here but  what else do we need to know on this  topic  before we let you go well when we're  doing this work around trans-racial  adoption  well and when we're doing it at the  highest level of engagement  it is a lesson in how to navigate and  face  differences of race for the world right  um that is not why  a lot of folks go into this trans-racial  adoption uh journey they want to have a  kid they want to raise a kid i get that  but when and i know plenty of families  that are doing an amazing job  at creating the right circumstances for  their kid to be seen  heard protected uh in environments i  mean they're literally just  changing their whole way of operating in  order to protect  this child and create a life um that  that can be  a beautiful uh one lived when that  happens at its highest level  that is a signal to how we all should be  thinking about  how to steer into complexity how to be  in a really really transformational  place around differences of race  um that is not their job that is not  those families job to be that  for us in the world